WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:04.320
Which King Kong movies are classics, and&nbsp;
which should have never left the island?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:07.520
Here's every King Kong movie ranked worst to best!

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:14.240
With a 0 percent Tomatometer score, King Kong&nbsp;
Lives is the worst King Kong movie, and it's also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:19.680
in the running for "Worst Movie Ever." Released&nbsp;
ten years after Dino De Laurentiis' King Kong in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:26.000
1976, King Kong Lives is the sequel nobody wanted&nbsp;
to the remake nobody asked for, with none of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.200
stars from the first film and a fraction of the&nbsp;
budget. Turns out King Kong survived his nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:36.640
2,000-foot fall from atop the World Trade Center,&nbsp;
but he needs an artificial heart to survive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:41.440
If that isn't bizarre enough, the film&nbsp;
also features Kong courting a female Kong.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:46.320
We can understand that De Laurentiis wanted to&nbsp;
make a sequel, albeit ten years after anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:51.600
cared, but was this really the best they could&nbsp;
do? King Kong Lives came out 53 years after the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:57.120
original King Kong, and yet its special effects&nbsp;
are less convincing. While King Kong's stop-motion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:02.240
animation was groundbreaking, King Kong Lives&nbsp;
opted for the more primitive filmmaking technique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:06.640
of putting a man in a gorilla suit. It's&nbsp;
little wonder King Kong Lives was a Raspberry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:12.880
Award nominee for "Worst Special Effects."King&nbsp;
Kong Lives bombed with $4.7 million worldwide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:15.680
effectively killing off&nbsp;
the character for 20 years.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:21.520
King Kong Lives came out ten&nbsp;
years after 1976's King Kong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:25.520
an eternity between sequels, but&nbsp;
Hollywood didn't always move so slowly.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:31.920
The original King Kong was released in New York&nbsp;
City on March 2, 1933, and its sequel Son of Kong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:40.320
came out on December 22, 1933. Yep. RKO Pictures&nbsp;
wrote, produced, edited, and released a sequel in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:45.200
nine months. But while you can respect their work&nbsp;
ethic, you can't respect the finished product.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:50.720
Son of Kong is just over 69 minutes long, so&nbsp;
it barely even qualifies as a feature film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:55.040
and it's so thrown together that it barely&nbsp;
qualifies as a movie-going experience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:58.960
As for the plot, Carl Denham, the&nbsp;
explorer/filmmaker from the original movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:03.600
has been sued into bankruptcy and returns to&nbsp;
Skull Island in the hopes of finding treasure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:08.000
to pay off his debts, but instead,&nbsp;
he finds a 25-foot-tall, albino,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:13.040
baby Kong instead. Son of Kong killed whatever&nbsp;
chance Kong had of being a multi-film series.

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:18.560
It was only natural a King Kong&nbsp;
remake would arrive in the 1970s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:22.880
the same era as The Towering Inferno,&nbsp;
The Poseidon Adventure, and Jaws.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:27.760
Naturally, the man to bring it to the screen was&nbsp;
Dino De Laurentiis. De Laurentiis didn't know the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:34.480
meaning of the word "small" and assembled a $24&nbsp;
million budget, about $108 million today, to top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:41.200
Spielberg's killer fish. The production spent $1.7&nbsp;
million alone on a 40-foot-tall Kong animatronic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:48.160
made with 3.5-tons of aluminum and 1,012 pounds&nbsp;
of Argentinian horsetails, but it was barely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:53.040
even used in the film. The eight-month shoot&nbsp;
featured 12-hour days, which tested the resolve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:58.400
of its stars Jeff Bridges and Charles Grodin, as&nbsp;
well as Jessica Lange in her first major part.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:02.160
"Oh god, I'm tired of thinking."

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:08.480
And since this was the 1970s, they couldn't just&nbsp;
make a movie — they also had to make a statement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:13.120
Grodin's evil oil exec travels to Kong's&nbsp;
island seeking black gold, but he exploits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:17.840
the famous big gorilla instead. While King Kong&nbsp;
mostly relied on the man-in-suit technique that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:23.520
looked less realistic than its 1933 predecessor,&nbsp;
the movie still snagged a Special Achievement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:30.880
Oscar for Special Effects. King Kong earned $52&nbsp;
million, making it the fourth biggest hit of 1976,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:36.080
but it was disappointing considering that was just&nbsp;
20 percent of what Jaws earned the year before.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:40.960
Today, Kong fans rightly view De Laurentiis'&nbsp;
King Kong as a monster disappointment.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:46.640
Following the enormous success of&nbsp;
King Kong vs. Godzilla in 1962,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:51.440
RKO allowed Toho Studios to produce a&nbsp;
sequel. Toho pitched a script pitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:56.640
the mighty ape against a giant lobster,&nbsp;
Ebirah, which RKO rejected. RKO now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:01.840
mandated that Toho co-produce the film&nbsp;
with American studio Rankin/Bass, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:07.040
was featuring King Kong in a popular Saturday&nbsp;
morning cartoon, The King Kong Show. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:11.600
there was already a script produced, which was&nbsp;
basically a big screen remake of the cartoon.

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:15.760
The result was King Kong Escapes. Toho&nbsp;
pulled out all the stops for this film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:19.680
assigning its top kaiju director,&nbsp;
Ishiro Honda, to the project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:23.760
alongside A-list actors&nbsp;
Akira Takarada and Mie Hama.

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:28.560
However, King Kong Escapes isn't a sequel&nbsp;
to the original Kong or even King Kong vs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:32.960
Godzilla. Instead, it exists in its own&nbsp;
universe. The film has a lot of plot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:38.320
but basically, King Kong rescues an expedition&nbsp;
from a dinosaur, is brought back to Tokyo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:43.600
and must stop a mad scientist from taking over&nbsp;
the world by battling his own mechanical double,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:46.720
Mechani-Kong. In other words,&nbsp;
you should absolutely watch it.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:52.080
King Kong vs. Godzilla screams no-brainer,&nbsp;
but its path to the big screen was anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:57.040
but straightforward. The stop-motion genius&nbsp;
behind the original King Kong, Willis O'Brien,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:00.080
wanted to pit Kong against Frankenstein's monster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:05.760
but Universal nixed the project. O'Brien's pitch&nbsp;
made its way to Toho Studios, but they wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:10.560
replace Frankenstein's monster with their own&nbsp;
creation, Godzilla, who'd been on a seven-year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:16.720
hiatus. A new script was written without O'Brien's&nbsp;
knowledge, and King Kong vs. Godzilla was born.

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:20.880
The film is a landmark sci-fi film that&nbsp;
launched the kitschy, colorful spirit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:26.320
that would define the previously somber Godzilla&nbsp;
series. The film was a massive hit in Japan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:31.200
and so it was rewritten to feature American&nbsp;
actors and distributed in the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:35.920
where it performed well. Contrary to popular&nbsp;
lore, there's no alternate Japanese ending where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:40.880
Godzilla swims off in victory instead of Kong.&nbsp;
However, the original Japanese version is much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:45.920
different from the American one, and it's really&nbsp;
more of a comedic satire. This version was elusive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:52.080
until Criterion Collection released it in 2019.&nbsp;
Whichever version you see, King Kong vs. Godzilla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:56.640
is a classic of the kaiju genre and one of&nbsp;
Kong's most famous cinematic appearances.

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:03.040
Gareth Edwards' Godzilla brought the Big&nbsp;
G back to American movie screens in 2014,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:09.600
defying even the highest expectations when it&nbsp;
opened to $93 million and earned $524 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:16.320
worldwide on a $160 million budget. Kaiju flicks&nbsp;
suddenly meant cash, and Legendary Pictures also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:21.600
hoped to capitalize on moviegoers' sudden appetite&nbsp;
for cinematic crossovers. Inspired by the success&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:26.480
of the Marvel Cinematic Universe, the company&nbsp;
launched its own series, the MonsterVerse. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:31.680
next film in the series would bring back arguably&nbsp;
the most famous monster in movies — King Kong.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:36.560
Kong: Skull Island assembled a stacked cast,&nbsp;
but the biggest star was Kong. Skull Island&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:41.680
wasn't a sequel or remake but a reboot, and it&nbsp;
ballooned the monster ape's height to 100 feet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:47.040
and dialed the cinematic spectacle up to 11. It&nbsp;
somehow managed to pay homage to Apocalypse Now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:51.280
and still give audiences what they paid&nbsp;
to see — big monsters fighting each other.

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:54.960
Kong: Skull Island received a&nbsp;
75 percent Tomatometer ranking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.960 --> 00:07:03.600
and earned $168 million domestically and $566&nbsp;
million worldwide on a $185 million budget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:08.400
making it the most successful movie so far in the&nbsp;
MonsterVerse. Kong: Skull Island is better than it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:13.520
had any right to be, with strong performances,&nbsp;
a breakneck pace, compelling characters you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:18.640
love to root for, and enough cinematic monster&nbsp;
carnage to create the perfect Saturday matinee.

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:26.160
Nearly 60 years after King Kong vs. Godzilla, the&nbsp;
big lizard got his rematch in 2021's Godzilla vs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:31.040
Kong. Besides being a much better movie than&nbsp;
the original, Godzilla vs. Kong could be more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:36.000
historically significant, too. That's pretty&nbsp;
impressive, considering King Kong vs. Godzilla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:41.040
was groundbreaking, launching the Toho Godzilla&nbsp;
series from a black-and-white, somber affair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:45.840
into the brightly colored, lighthearted "Godzilla&nbsp;
vs. fill-in-the-blank" format we know and love.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:50.160
However, Godzilla vs. Kong received the&nbsp;
widest theater count of the pandemic era&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:54.880
while also being released on streamer HBO&nbsp;
Max, which for many analysts signaled the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:59.200
beginning of a return to "normalcy." You&nbsp;
couldn't find a more unlikely candidate.

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:03.920
Godzilla: King of the Monsters underwhelmed&nbsp;
during the infamous summer of 2019, casting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:09.280
the MonsterVerse's future in doubt. Godzilla vs.&nbsp;
Kong seemed destined to disappoint ... then the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:14.240
first trailer dropped and became the fourth most&nbsp;
liked trailer ever. In some strange way, Godzilla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:19.360
vs. Kong became the movie audiences needed.&nbsp;
As critic Shirley Li wrote in The Atlantic,

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:24.240
"It's kind of admirable how aggressively&nbsp;
Godzilla vs. Kong eschews any grander meaning."

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:29.360
In an era when every big, dumb blockbuster thinks&nbsp;
adding "darkness" and "meaning" makes it better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:34.320
Godzilla vs. Kong succeeds because it's just&nbsp;
a big, dumb blockbuster. Seriously, if you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:38.400
looking for epic fights between towering beasts,&nbsp;
or if you just really want to see an oversized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:43.280
gorilla wielding an axe, well, Adam Wingard's&nbsp;
action flick is definitely the film for you.

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:46.720
Whatever Godzilla vs. Kong&nbsp;
ultimately means for movie history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:50.000
it already has become one of the&nbsp;
best King Kong movies of all time.

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:56.400
2005's King Kong isn't really a remake so much&nbsp;
as it's the biggest example of fan fiction in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:01.520
cinematic history. As Roger Ebert put it in&nbsp;
his four-star review, King Kong was, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:05.840
"like the flowering of all the possibilities&nbsp;
in the original classic film." Director Peter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:10.480
Jackson loved King Kong ever since he was a&nbsp;
kid, and with his newfound creative freedom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:14.000
coming off The Lord of the Rings trilogy,&nbsp;
he sought to bring the big ape back in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:19.120
biggest way imaginable. But as with every other&nbsp;
King Kong movie, the path was anything but easy.

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:25.200
A pre-LOTR Peter Jackson pitched a King Kong&nbsp;
remake to Universal execs in the mid-1990s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:27.440
While the studio was on board with his vision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:32.080
execs balked when they realized a new Godzilla&nbsp;
and Mighty Joe Young were coming out first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:36.800
and they feared what might happen if their movie&nbsp;
showed up late to the party. A heartbroken Jackson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:42.480
took refuge in J.R.R. Tolkien's masterwork,&nbsp;
raising his stock considerably. Jackson eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:48.640
got a Titanic-sized $207 million budget and&nbsp;
an epic three-hour runtime. It was a lot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:53.120
but after the success of the Lord of the Rings&nbsp;
trilogy, Universal figured it was a bargain.

00:09:53.120 --> 00:10:01.120
While King Kong received an 84 percent Tomatometer&nbsp;
score, its $218 million domestic and $562 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:06.880
worldwide box office gross underwhelmed based&nbsp;
on its budget. Is it too long? Maybe. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:11.680
while Kong's epic length proved too much for&nbsp;
many moviegoers, Kong diehards believed Peter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:16.640
Jackson's love letter to the original couldn't&nbsp;
last long enough. After all, the relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:22.080
between the big ape and Naomi Watt's Ann Darrow&nbsp;
feels genuinely sweet, and the fantastic sequence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:26.240
where Kong battles a bunch of dinosaurs is&nbsp;
the kind of scene that movies were made for.

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:33.440
King Kong is one of the greatest movies ever.&nbsp;
Period. Big-budget genre blockbusters dominate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:38.240
movies today, and King Kong was first. Only&nbsp;
Star Wars comes close to its influence on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:43.040
special effects, and at nearly 100 years&nbsp;
old, King Kong is still the best stop-motion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:47.520
movie ever. The film is a 100-minute&nbsp;
highlight reel of iconic movie moments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:51.440
from "the beauty" Fay Wray screaming in&nbsp;
terror at "the beast" to the mighty ape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.440 --> 00:10:56.160
smashing through Skull Island's impenetrable&nbsp;
doors to the heartbroken King Kong taking his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.160 --> 00:11:01.040
last stand atop the Empire State Building. King&nbsp;
Kong is truly a triumph of the imagination,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:05.280
the kind of cinematic spectacle that could&nbsp;
only be made in the Golden Age of Hollywood.

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:10.800
"Oh no, it wasn't the airplanes.&nbsp;
It was beauty killed the beast."

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:16.000
But the backstory of King Kong is almost as&nbsp;
amazing as the film itself. Globetrotting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:22.000
adventurer Merian C. Cooper developed projects for&nbsp;
RKO Pictures' chief David O. Selznick, and he came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:26.720
up with the idea of a giant gorilla picture. He&nbsp;
was originally going to have a real-life gorilla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:32.080
fight a Komodo dragon, but when that idea became&nbsp;
untenable, he stumbled onto a dinosaur project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:38.240
called Creation from the stop-motion mastermind&nbsp;
behind 1925's The Lost World, Willis O'Brien.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:43.360
Inspired, Cooper thought, "What if the gorilla&nbsp;
fought dinosaurs?" And a masterpiece was born.

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:50.480
King Kong cost $500,000, equivalent to $10 million&nbsp;
today, which was a fortune at the time, but it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:55.840
worth it. King Kong was a huge hit, and the eighth&nbsp;
wonder of the world still inspires awe today.

